        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9160/0 Fibrous papule</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127395,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F23.Y Other specified benign dermal fibrous or fibrohistiocytic neoplasms</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127396,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> angiofibroma.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127397,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127398,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The most common sites include the nose and central face {30028760}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127399,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous papule typically manifests as a dome-shaped, sessile papule that is solitary and sporadic in the vast majority of cases. Multiple (syndromic) fibrous papules may be associated with tuberous sclerosis, and less commonly with Birt&ndash;Hogg&ndash;Dub&eacute; syndrome or multiple endocrine neoplasia type&nbsp;1 {30246432; 16021156; 9236523}. There is a tendency for multiple fibrous papules to occur in early childhood in tuberous sclerosis, whereas those associated with Birt&ndash;Hogg&ndash;Dub&eacute; syndrome or multiple endocrine neoplasia type&nbsp;1 are usually seen in adults {30246432}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127400,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous papules are most commonly seen in middle-aged adults {30028760}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127401,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127402,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multiple fibrous papules in tuberous sclerosis are thought to occur due to constitutional activation of the mTOR pathway due to germline mutations in <em>TSC1</em> or <em>TSC2</em> (tuberous sclerosis), and similar mechanisms have been suggested for the pathogenesis of sporadic fibrous papules {24558502}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127403,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Fibrous papules manifest as well-circumscribed papular lesions with a tan or white cut surface.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 127404,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 310,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Classic fibrous papule has a dome-shaped configuration with dilated blood vessels within the dermis, surrounded by fibrotic or collagenized stroma with spindle-shaped or stellate fibroblasts {15953376}. The overlying epidermis may show melanocytic hyperplasia {32496596}. Histological patterns of fibrous papule may be hypercellular, pigmented, pleomorphic, clear cell, granular cell, epithelioid, or inflammatory {15953376}.</p>\r\n<h6>Differential diagnosis</h6>\r\n<p>The differential diagnosis includes fibroma, melanocytic naevus (pigmented or epithelioid pattern), atypical fibroxanthoma (hypercellular or pleomorphic pattern), balloon cell naevus or xanthoma (clear cell pattern), and granular cell tumour (granular pattern).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T09:45:07"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
